--- read-back
You buy used construction machines at online auctions and resell them to contractors. You personally decide which listings to chase and bid on, with no sign-off, inside a capped inventory and the quarterly gross margin the owner watches. The choice is to bid or pass, and it is made from photos, the hour meter and the seller's note, with the machine hundreds of miles away and often only a few days left.
The other parties are the auction house, which wants a high hammer price and does not volunteer problems; the seller, who wants the machine gone and holds the maintenance history; other bidders, usually a handful, with contractors tending to bid higher because they will run the machine; and the owner, who reviews quarterly margin.
What you can see at the start is the photos, the hour meter, the seller's note, the auction house's condition score, the year, and whether the seller is a fleet or a contractor. What you cannot see is the true condition: the hydraulic pump, final drives, undercarriage and whether the hour meter was swapped. Roughly 4 in 10 machines with no service records have a major fault, against about 1 in 10 for fleet sellers with records. Service records can be requested and arrive about half the time, in about a day.
You can bid, pass, or get a pre-bid inspection for about 550 (450 to 700), ready in a couple of working days. The inspection catches about 7 in 10 major hidden faults and wrongly flags about 1 in 10 good machines. An oil sample adds about 90 and three days and catches about one more fault in ten; a seller can top up with heavy oil to fool it. The inspector is shared, about two a week. The buyer's premium is a fixed 10 percent of the hammer price, and transport is about 2400 (1800 to 3500). A winning bid is binding: backing out loses the deposit and the bidding rights.
A good outcome is a sound machine that sells after cleanup: about three thousand of repairs, then eight to twelve thousand of gain, with cash sitting three to six weeks. A bad outcome is a major hidden fault: repairs of fifteen to thirty thousand, a loss of usually eight to ten thousand after resale whatever the price, and cash sitting a couple of months. Carrying cost on the money is about 9 percent a year, roughly 500 a month on a 65,000 machine. The count is resale minus hammer, premium, transport, repairs and inspection, and cash stuck in the machine cannot go into the next one. Keep cash under ninety days.
The hard part is the trade-off: inspecting costs a few hundred and a couple of days and can lose quick deals, while skipping risks a bad buy that wipes out a couple of good ones. The rule you gave is: fleet seller with records under about 45,000, bid without inspecting; over 50,000 or with no records, inspect or pass, never bid blind; between 45,000 and 50,000 is the gray zone, a feel rather than a count, about 3 in 10 listings. Seller type and records decide whether to inspect more than the dollars do. A listing warning such as a pump whine can skip the inspection and go straight to pass.
Cases differ mainly by records, seller type and price. Usually 40,000 to 80,000, sometimes well outside. About 4 in 10 listings have records. Cheaper machines tend to have higher hours. Your professional rule for a fleet with records under 45,000 is to bid up to roughly 75 percent of expected resale, and for others to inspect or pass. The top bid is the lower of 75 percent of expected resale and expected resale minus about 12,000 minus expected repairs; the two cross near 60,000 resale. A newcomer who bids up to book value less about 10 percent works about six times in ten, and fails on machines with no records and forgets the premium and freight.
Three worked cases: a 2017 excavator with no records, clean inspection, hammer 60,500 and expected resale about 78,000, bid and won; an unknown seller at 70,000 against book 95,000 with a pump whine, passed without inspecting; a contractor with partial records at about 48,000 with two days left and no inspector, passed as a close call. The one loose end is the 2017 excavator's final cleanup cost, not yet pulled from the invoice. Please check this summary and correct anything I have wrong.
